1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,400
[wind howling]

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,800
<i>The lamp went out at midnight,
and nobody on the island noticed.</i>

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,900
- You're late again, Marta.
- The ferry was late. I wasn't.

4
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:12,100
{\an8}KEEPER'S LOG, DAY 412

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,000
I told the harbour office last week that

6
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,600
the generator wouldn't survive another storm.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:19,200
And?

8
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:22,300
And they sent me a form
to fill in. In triplicate.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:24,000
♪♪

10
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:27,000
ILSE: Did you check the fuel line
before you blamed the generator?

11
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,000
- Twice.
- And the spare bulbs?

12
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:31,800
We have three left. Three bulbs
between us and forty fishing boats.

13
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:33,300
1912.

14
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,900
That's the year stamped on the lens.
It has outlived four keepers.

15
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:39,800
(SIGHS) It will not outlive this winter
unless somebody pays for the repairs.

16
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,200
<i>Harbour office to North Rock, come in.</i>

17
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,500
North Rock here. Go ahead.

18
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:53,100
<i>We've got a trawler overdue,
last seen heading for your reef.</i>

19
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:55,000
How long overdue?

20
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:56,600
<i>Six hours.</i>

21
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:59,900
Marta, get the hand lamp
and every flare we own.

22
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:02,700
- You can't light a reef with a hand lamp.
- Watch me.

23
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:05,400
[thunder rumbles]

24
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,800
If the generator dies while they're
out there, I'm never forgiving that office.

25
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,700
Then let's make sure it doesn't die.

26
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:14,100
Hold this. And don't let go.
